DCL (Data Control Language): 
==============================
    DCL is used to control database security.

    It decides who can access the database and what they can do.

    ●It is related to security issues
    
    List of  DCL commands: 
    
    • GRANT:This command gives users access privileges to the database.(Granting permission to access the database)
     EX : GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON Employee TO user1;

    • REVOKE:This command withdraws the user’s access privileges given by using the GRANT command.(It is used to cancel previously granted permission(block))

     EX :  REVOKE SELECT ON Employee FROM user1;


    When is GRANT used?
    ---------------------
        When a new user joins
        When someone’s role changes
        When you want to control access

    When is REVOKE used?
    ---------------------
        Employee leaves the company
        User misuses data
        Role no longer requires access


Transaction Control Language
==============================
    TCL commands can only use with DML commands like INSERT, DELETE and UPDATE only.
    If any of the tasks fail, the transaction fails. Therefore, a transaction has only two results: success or failure.
    When do we use TCL?
    If any of the tasks fail, we use TCL to undo all the changes or only the required changes.

    • COMMIT:
    ----------
         Commit command is used to save all the transactions to the database.(Google pay)
        Syntax:
        • COMMIT;  
        Example:
        • DELETE FROM CUSTOMERS  
        • WHERE AGE = 25;  
        • COMMIT;  
    
    • ROLLBACK: 
     -------------

        • This command helps you undo recent changes in a database, It is also used with SAVEPOINT command to jump to a savepoint in an ongoing transaction.
        
        • Rollbacks a transaction in case of any error occurs.
        
        • If we have used the UPDATE command to make some changes into the database, and realise that those changes were not required, then we can use the ROLLBACK command to rollback those changes, if they were not commited using the COMMIT command.
    
        • Following is rollback command's syntax,
        • 
        • ROLLBACK TO savepoint_name;
        • 
        • Example:
        • DELETE FROM CUSTOMERS  
        • WHERE AGE = 25;  
        • ROLLBACK;  
    
    🔹 When to use ROLLBACK?
    ---------------------------
        Error occurs

        Payment fails

        User cancels operation



    • SAVEPOINT command
    ----------------------
    
        • SAVEPOINT: It is used to roll the transaction back to a certain point without rolling back the entire transaction.(downloading a file)
        -->  SAVEPOINT is used only when partial rollback is required.
        • SAVEPOINT command is used to temporarily save a transaction so that you can rollback to that point whenever required.
        • 
        • Following is savepoint command's syntax,
        • 
        • SAVEPOINT savepoint_name;
        Syntax:
        • SAVEPOINT SAVEPOINT_NAME;  


    Example : 

        In our project, Initially we used transactions while placing an order.
        First, we create the order.
        Then, we reduce the product stock.
        After that, we take the payment.
        All these steps are executed inside a single transaction.

        If all steps are successful, we commit the transaction and save the data permanently.
        If any step fails, like payment failure, we roll back the transaction so that no partial data is saved.

        This helps us maintain data consistency and avoid incorrect orders or stock issues


