    Ex with SAVEPOINT : 
    ---------------------
    ✔ Order stays
    ❌ Stock undone
    ❌ Payment undone



    sequelize.transaction({ transaction: t }, async (t2) => {})
    👉 Sequelize creates a SAVEPOINT

    Internally DB does:

    SAVEPOINT sequelize_savepoint_1;





    const placeOrder = async (req, res) => {
      const t = await sequelize.transaction(); // MAIN transaction

      try {
        // 1️⃣ Create order
        const order = await Order.create(
          { userId: 1, totalAmount: 1000 },
          { transaction: t }
        );

        // 2️⃣ SAVEPOINT starts here (nested transaction)
        await sequelize.transaction({ transaction: t }, async (t2) => {
        
          // Reduce stock
          const product = await Product.findByPk(1, { transaction: t2 });
          product.stock -= 1;
          await product.save({ transaction: t2 });

          // Simulate payment failure
          const paymentSuccess = false;

          if (!paymentSuccess) {
            throw new Error("Payment failed");
          }

          // Save payment
          await Payment.create(
            { orderId: order.id, status: "SUCCESS" },
            { transaction: t2 }
          );
        });

        // If everything is OK
        await t.commit();
        res.json({ message: "Order placed successfully" });

      } catch (error) {
        await t.rollback();
        res.status(500).json({ message: error.message });
      }
    };


Data Query Language
======================
    DQL is used to fetch the data from the database.
    
    It uses only one command:
    
    • SELECT
    
    a. SELECT: This is the same as the projection operation of relational algebra. It is used to select the attribute based on the condition described by WHERE clause.
    
    Syntax:
    • SELECT expressions    
    • FROM TABLES    
    • WHERE conditions;  
    For example:
    • SELECT emp_name  
    • FROM employee  
    • WHERE age > 20;  
    
